---
/**
 * foodcraft / FooterBL — THE CREAM CLOSING BAND. The design's last element,
 * reproduced: a --paper-2 ground under one --line hairline, 58px of air, then a
 * minmax(0,1.6fr) / minmax(0,1fr) / minmax(0,1fr) grid at a 40px gap — the
 * wordmark lock with its rust town tag, a blurb and one quiet rust action; a
 * page-links column; a contact column — closed by a hairline and a 22px-deep
 * colophon row of 10px caps at .24em.
 *
 * ROLE: the last place a visitor looks for the name, the phone and the address
 * before they give up. So every cell here is real owner data or it is absent.
 *
 * EDITABLE HOOKS — every visible string in this file, and nothing else. Each is
 * declared in components/editor/genericContentSchema.ts, so each is a hook a
 * sidebar input actually owns (a data-field on any other path is a dead hook):
 *   footer.brand              — the 24px Fraunces wordmark, and the colophon's
 *                               identity half (HeaderBL's wordmark too: one
 *                               field, three places, on purpose)
 *   footer.locality           — the 9px rust town tag on the wordmark baseline
 *   footer.blurb              — the standing line under the lock
 *   footer.explore.title      — the PAGE-LINKS column heading (see decision 1)
 *   footer.visit.title        — the CONTACT column heading (see decision 1)
 *   navbar_links.${i}.label   — one page-column link's label
 *   navbar_links.${i}.href    — that link's target (data-href-field)
 *   footer.cta.text                — the rust action's label (see decision 4)
 *   footer.cta.href                — its target (data-href-field)
 *   location.phone            — the tel: row (data-field only, decision 7)
 *   contact.email             — the mailto: row (decision 8)
 *   location.address          — the address line, and the colophon's other half
 *
 * DERIVED here, never authored here:
 *   · the wordmark falls back to layout.businessName when footer.brand is
 *     blank — the same name out of the same submission, not an invention.
 *   · the blurb falls back to footer.brand_blurb, the schema's own alias.
 *   · phone, email and address fall back to layout.contact.* — which IS
 *     content.contact, so an owner who filled the footer group's own
 *     "Email"/"Address" boxes still gets those rows, and the page can never
 *     print two different numbers.
 *   · the tel: href is built from the phone's own digits; the mailto: href is
 *     built from the email. Neither prefix is ever stored.
 *   · every in-page href is resolved by onPage() against the ids PageBL really
 *     renders — the helper is byte-identical to HeaderBL's, so the rail and the
 *     footer can never send a visitor to two different places.
 *   · `i` on a page link is the ORIGINAL, pre-filter index (R6), so dropping an
 *     unreachable row 2 cannot misdirect an edit into row 3.
 *   · the colophon's address is FLATTENED to one line (newlines to commas) to
 *     fit a single row of tracked caps — which is why that node, and only that
 *     node, carries data-derived: editorBridge's live ed:update would otherwise
 *     paint the raw multi-line scalar into a caps row. The COLUMN's address is
 *     not derived: it is the stored string verbatim, broken by
 *     `white-space: pre-line`, so an inline edit there stays truthful.
 *
 * NOT RENDERED, deliberately:
 *   · THE DESIGN'S OWN COPY. "Kalinaw Coffee", "Cebu", "This page", "Get in
 *     touch", "Menu", "Our story", "Gallery", "FAQ", "Contact us", the blurb and
 *     "12 Salinas Drive, Lahug, Cebu City" are the DESIGNER filling in their own
 *     template. Not one of those words is in this file. A café that supplied no
 *     nav links gets no links column, not the designer's four.
 *   · "PRICES IN PHILIPPINE PESOS" — the design's right-hand colophon cell. It
 *     is a claim about how this business quotes, and no hook in this file's
 *     brief carries it (footer.notes exists in the schema but is not one of the
 *     paths this component was given). So the cell is simply absent and the
 *     colophon renders as one left-aligned line. Flagged for review: if the
 *     right cell is wanted back, footer.notes is the field that would feed it.
 *   · a column heading with no column under it, a wordmark with no name, a
 *     blurb with no text, a link with no label or no reachable target, a
 *     colophon with neither name nor address, and the whole footer when all of
 *     the above are empty. EVERY guard sits outside its tag, so a blank value
 *     removes the ELEMENT and not just its text.
 *   · social chips: the design draws none.
 *   · an hours block: the design's footer has none; LocationBL owns the hours.
 *   · .reveal. The footer is the page terminus; fading in the last thing on
 *     screen buys nothing and risks a footer that never un-hides.
 *   · any star row, rating or numeral — there is nothing on this band that
 *     could print a claim the owner did not make.
 *
 * DECISIONS a reader would otherwise take for an oversight
 *
 * 1. WHICH HEADING FIELD FEEDS WHICH COLUMN — THE ONE THING TO REVIEW.
 *    This file's brief pairs footer.visit.title with the LINKS column and
 *    footer.explore.title with the CONTACT column. genericContentSchema.ts
 *    declares the opposite, in its own owner-facing labels:
 *      footer.explore.title → label "Links column heading",   placeholder "This page"
 *      footer.visit.title   → label "Contact column heading",  placeholder "Get in touch"
 *    — and those two placeholders are this design's two headings verbatim.
 *    Following the brief would put the sidebar box an owner reads as "Contact
 *    column heading" in charge of the heading over Menu / Our story / Gallery /
 *    FAQ, on this one template, while FooterBK and FooterBJ bind it the other
 *    way. The schema's own naming wins here. Flag it if the brief is right.
 *
 * 2. THE COLUMN HEADING IS NOT .kick, THOUGH IT LOOKS LIKE ONE. Every .kick
 *    declaration in PageBL sits inside :where(), at zero specificity, while the
 *    wrapper's `html[data-page="BL"] :where(h1,h2,h3,h4)` rule scores (0,1,0)
 *    and sets font-family, weight, colour, letter-spacing, line-height,
 *    text-transform and margin. On an <h4>, .kick would therefore contribute
 *    literally nothing — the heading would render in Fraunces at 400 in --ink.
 *    So .fb-h restates the eyebrow treatment at scoped specificity instead.
 *    It stays an <h4> because a real heading gives the two columns structure a
 *    screen-reader user can navigate; the design's plain <div> does not.
 *
 * 3. THE ARROW after the brand column's action is CSS generated content, not
 *    markup. editorBridge writes an inline edit back through innerHTML, so a
 *    glyph inside the element could end up stored in footer.cta.text; a pseudo-
 *    element cannot. It carries no margin: .linkc is an inline-flex box, so the
 *    pseudo-element is a flex ITEM and the primitive's own `gap: .5em` already
 *    spaces it — a margin would stack on top and open twice the design's gap.
 *
 * 4. THE ACTION IS DRAWN ONCE, NOT TWICE. The design draws contactLabel in BOTH
 *    the brand column and the contact column, and that reads correctly there
 *    because the designer set contactLabel to "Contact us" and headed the
 *    column "Get in touch". On a real site both come from
 *    lib/derive-content-defaults.ts, which seeds navCtaText to "Get in touch" —
 *    so ONE field printed the same three words twice, six lines apart, and
 *    FooterBK had to be fixed for exactly that. The rule here:
 *      · the action rides the BRAND column whenever that column exists on its
 *        own merits (a wordmark, a town tag or a blurb);
 *      · it falls to the CONTACT column only when the brand column does not
 *        render at all — which is where the design also draws it;
 *      · and never in the contact column when it merely repeats that column's
 *        heading, compared case- and whitespace-insensitively, because "Get In
 *        Touch" is the same stutter.
 *    (FooterBK's version of this test folded ctaText into its own brand-column
 *    gate, which made the contact-column branch unreachable in every case. Here
 *    the brand gate counts only lock and blurb, so the fallback is live.)
 *    Nothing is lost when the action is suppressed: the header rail still
 *    carries the pill, and phone, email and address are distinct channels.
 *
 *    The pair hooked is footer.cta.text / footer.cta.href, this section's own, not
 *    hero.cta1: components/editor/editorBridge.ts resolves a Blocks toggle by
 *    counting hooks of that prefix inside closest('section,footer'), so a
 *    footer carrying hero.* hooks becomes a candidate for "hide the Hero
 *    block". A footer.* hook belongs to the FOOTER block instead, and
 *    that block's own toggle already resolves to this component, so the
 *    pair stays toggle-safe. HeaderBL keeps navCtaText, which is now
 *    a different field from this one — that split is the point.
 *
 * 5. THE TWO location.* HOOKS HERE ARE SAFE under that same weighted
 *    resolution: this footer holds 2 paths across 3 nodes (phone, the column
 *    address, the colophon address), while the Location section holds its own
 *    tag, headline, address, phone and hours — so "hide Location" still resolves
 *    to Location. Recorded, not fixed; it is a shared-file concern. The
 *    footer's own toggle resolves here easily (6 footer.* hits against
 *    HeaderBL's 2, and a <header> is not even in the ancestor list).
 *
 * 6. NO .btn IN THIS FILE, AT ALL. lib/genericThemeOverrides.ts emits
 *    `footer .btn { color: <on-ink> !important }`, so a .btn on this CREAM band
 *    would take a label colour picked for an ink ground. The design's footer
 *    has no button anyway — a 13px text action and 14px rows — so the actions
 *    are .linkc and plain links, which the engine never touches. None of the
 *    forbidden names (.nav-cta, .btn-primary, .btn-ghost, .testi, .cta-band,
 *    .trust) appears either (R4).
 *
 * 7. THE tel: ANCHOR CARRIES data-field ONLY, no data-href-field. Its href is
 *    derived from the field's own value, so a href hook there would write
 *    "tel:+63…" straight back into the phone box. Same for the mailto: row.
 *
 * 8. THE EMAIL ROW IS AN ADDITION. The design draws none — its demo café took
 *    enquiries on Messenger — but contact.email is schema-declared in the very
 *    group this footer belongs to, and a real business that supplies one had it
 *    appear NOWHERE on the page. It takes the design's own 14px row treatment,
 *    so nothing new is invented visually.
 *
 * 9. PHONE AND EMAIL ARE UNDERLINED, THE ADDRESS IS NOT. They sit directly
 *    above a static address line that is the same family, size and weight and
 *    differs only in colour, and colour alone does not distinguish a link
 *    (WCAG 1.4.1). text-decoration adds no box, so thickening it on hover
 *    cannot shift the rows. Deliberately NOT applied to the page column: those
 *    are wayfinding links in a list, already unambiguous by position and
 *    heading, and the design draws them bare.
 *
 * 10. THE LINK HOVER IS --accent-deep. It is the design's OWN stated hover —
 *     its stylesheet declares a:hover{color:#8E3F14}, which is --accent-deep.
 *     In the demo that rule is shadowed by the inline colour on every footer
 *     link, so the designer's page happens to render no hover change: an
 *     artefact of inline-style specificity, not a decision.
 *
 * 11. THE ROWS ARE BLOCKS, NOT THE DESIGN'S <p>. The design uses
 *     `<p style="margin:0 0 9px">` per row with `margin:0` on the last, which
 *     lands a ~27px pitch. Here each row is a block anchor at 14px/1.5 with 3px
 *     of padding either side — the same 27px pitch, the whole row is the tap
 *     target, and there is no trailing margin to dangle above the hairline.
 *
 * 12. THE BRAND ACTION SITS ~11px LOWER THAN DRAWN. The blurb keeps the
 *     design's 18px bottom margin verbatim, and .linkc's own min-height:44px —
 *     the wrapper's tap-target floor — adds about 11px of optical space under
 *     it. Trimming the margin to compensate would make the file disagree with
 *     the design for a correction that changes the moment .linkc does, so the
 *     design's value ships and the accessibility floor stands.
 *
 * COLOUR — wrapper tokens only, no literal hex anywhere (R1). Every value in
 * this design's footer maps to a token with the SAME value:
 *   #F7EFE1 --paper-2 (ground) · #E9DFCF --line (top hairline) · #241A12 --ink
 *   (wordmark) · #B4541F --accent (town tag, headings, the action) · #6B5C4E
 *   --ink-soft (blurb, link rows) · #948373 --muted (address line) · #8E3F14
 *   --accent-deep (the design's stated hover).
 * TWO have no exact token, and both resolve the same way — to the token that
 * TRAVELS with the ground they sit on, because buildOverrideCss remaps
 * --paper-2, --line, --ink-soft and --muted but leaves --line-2, --line-3 and
 * --line-btn baked:
 *   · #E5D9C6, the grid's closing hairline → var(--line). --line-btn (#E0D3C0)
 *     is a hair nearer numerically, but it is the design's BUTTON hairline and
 *     the engine never moves it — on a dark scheme it would draw a bright cream
 *     line across a dark footer. A 4/6/9-unit difference is imperceptible; a
 *     baked hairline is not.
 *   · #A2907E, the colophon caps → var(--muted) (#948373), via the wrapper's
 *     own .mono primitive, which is exactly this treatment. It is slightly
 *     DARKER than drawn, so the design's weakest ratio improves rather than
 *     degrades, and it moves with the ground under a scheme pick.
 *
 * CONTRAST on this band's --paper-2 ground: ink 13.5 · ink-soft 6.2 · accent
 * 4.9 · accent-deep ~6.6 · muted 3.6. The last is BELOW AA and is the design's
 * own value, used only on 10px colophon caps and the address line that the
 * link row above repeats. It is the owner's palette and the owner's call; the
 * file ships the design's rather than re-deriving it, exactly as PageBL records.
 *
 * Visibility: PageBL gates this file on visibility.footerSection — never here.
 */
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any; }
const { content, layout } = Astro.props;
const c = (content as any) || {};
const lay = (layout as any) || {};
const f = c.footer || {};

// ── ANCHOR GUARD — byte-identical to HeaderBL's, so the rail and the footer can
// never send a visitor to different places. lib/derive-content-defaults.ts
// hard-codes nav links to '#about', '#services', '#why', '#work', '#visit' and
// navCtaHref to '#visit'; lib/astro-builder.ts falls back to '#about',
// '#services', '#gallery', '#contact'. Most of those ids no design renders.
// Resolve every in-page href against what PageBL ACTUALLY renders: keep it if
// the id exists, remap it when this design carries the same material under
// another name, otherwise fall back — and the links column passes '' as the
// fallback, because a dead entry in a wayfinding list is worse than a missing
// one. Off-page hrefs (tel:, mailto:, http(s), anything without a leading '#')
// pass through untouched.
const PAGE_ANCHORS = new Set(['menu', 'about', 'gallery', 'location', 'faq', 'main']);
// This design renames four derived targets: the services block IS the menu
// (rooms lands there too), '#about' is the café's own story (host and why are
// the same block here), '#work' is the photo wall, and '#visit' / '#contact'
// both mean the location block — address, map and opening hours. A booking link
// has no checkout to reach on this page, so it lands on <main>.
const ANCHOR_ALIAS: Record<string, string> = {
  book: 'main',
  contact: 'location',
  host: 'about',
  rooms: 'menu',
  services: 'menu',
  visit: 'location',
  why: 'about',
  work: 'gallery',
};
const onPage = (href: string | undefined, fallback: string): string => {
  if (!href) return fallback;
  if (!href.startsWith('#')) return href;
  const id = href.slice(1);
  if (PAGE_ANCHORS.has(id)) return href;
  const alias = ANCHOR_ALIAS[id];
  return alias && PAGE_ANCHORS.has(alias) ? `#${alias}` : fallback;
};

// The wordmark. `||`, never `??`, and identical to HeaderBL's resolution:
// genericContentSchema gives footer.brand fallbackPaths ['business_name'] and
// ContentFieldsAuto's readWithFallbacks treats '' as empty, so a CLEARED brand
// box still shows the business name in the sidebar. With `??` an empty string
// would be a value and the page would go wordmark-less while the sidebar
// displayed a name. 'Your Business' once shipped as a real customer's own mark;
// with nothing supplied the lock is omitted instead of inventing a name.
const brand = String(f.brand || lay.businessName || '').trim();
// One field, three consumers (the rail, this lock, the map caption). Nothing
// derives it, so on a first build it is simply absent rather than guessed out
// of the address.
const locality = String(f.locality ?? '').trim();
// footer.brand_blurb is the schema's declared alias for the same box.
const blurb = String(f.blurb || f.brand_blurb || '').trim();

// This section's own action (see the note below). '#location' is
// the fallback because that is where this design puts the address, the map and
// the opening hours; there is no checkout to send a booking link to.
// THIS FOOTER'S OWN action. footer.cta.* is read FIRST and falls back to
// navCtaText / navCtaHref — the HEADER's field, which is exactly what these two
// lines read before — so a site that never filled the footer's box in renders
// byte-identically and no live footer loses its link. The hooks below spell
// footer.cta.*, so the owner's first edit lands there and this action's label,
// link and colour part company with the header pill's from then on. That split
// is the whole point: one field used to print "Get in touch" in the top bar and
// again down here, and an owner asked why.
const ctaText = String(f.cta?.text ?? '').trim() || String(c.navCtaText ?? '').trim();
const ctaHref = onPage(f.cta?.href || c.navCtaHref, '#location');
// Computed, never literal, and ONE conditional per attribute — an owner may
// point this at #location or a tel:, and forcing those into a new tab is wrong.
const ctaExternal = /^https?:/i.test(ctaHref);

// Column headings — bound, not hardcoded, and guarded, so an owner who clears
// the box gets a column with no heading rather than the designer's word. See
// decision 1 for which field feeds which column.
const pageTitle = String(f.explore?.title ?? '').trim();
const contactTitle = String(f.visit?.title ?? '').trim();

// The page column reads layout.navLinks — the same array HeaderBL reads, which
// lib/astro-builder.ts maps from content.navbar_links — while hooking
// `navbar_links.${i}.*`, the path the sidebar owns. Reading content directly
// here would let the rail and the footer disagree after a builder fallback.
const pageLinks: Array<{ label: string; href: string; i: number }> =
  (Array.isArray(lay.navLinks) ? lay.navLinks : [])
    .map((l: any, i: number) => ({ label: String(l?.label ?? '').trim(), href: onPage(l?.href, ''), i }))
    .filter((l: { label: string; href: string }) => l.label && l.href);

// Contact rows. layout.contact IS content.contact (astro-builder passes it
// through with a display-formatted phone), so these fallbacks also pick up the
// footer group's own "Email" / "Address" boxes, and no two sections on this
// page can print different numbers or different addresses.
const phone = String(c.location?.phone || lay.contact?.phone || '').trim();
const email = String(c.contact?.email || lay.contact?.email || '').trim();
const address = String(c.location?.address || lay.contact?.address || '').trim();
// Built, never stored (decision 7).
const phoneDigits = phone.replace(/[^0-9+]/g, '');
// Colophon identity line: the design prints one line, and location.address is a
// multi-line textarea. Flattened for THIS row only (hence data-derived on it).
const addressFlat = address.replace(/\s*\n+\s*/g, ', ');

// ── GATES — BODY content only (R5). The two column headings never count: a
// heading is not a column, and one printed over nothing is the exact failure
// the rule exists for. footer.brand / footer.blurb / navbar_links / the header CTA
// ARE seeded by derive-content-defaults, but from the owner's own submitted
// name, city and business type — that is their data, not an invented headline.
const hasBrandCol = Boolean(brand || locality || blurb);
// Decision 4: one action, and never a stutter against the heading beside it.
const norm = (v: string): string => v.replace(/\s+/g, ' ').trim().toLowerCase();
const brandShowsCta = Boolean(ctaText) && hasBrandCol;
const showContactCta =
  Boolean(ctaText) && !brandShowsCta && norm(ctaText) !== norm(contactTitle);
const hasContactCol = Boolean(showContactCta || phone || email || address);
const hasGrid = hasBrandCol || pageLinks.length > 0 || hasContactCol;
const hasColophon = Boolean(brand || addressFlat);
---
{(hasGrid || hasColophon) && (
<footer class="fb">
  <div class="wrap">
    {hasGrid && (
      <div class="fb-grid">
        {hasBrandCol && (
          <div class="fb-brand">
            {(brand || locality) && (
              <div class="fb-lock">
                {brand && <span class="fb-mark" data-field="footer.brand">{brand}</span>}
                {locality && <span class="fb-town" data-field="footer.locality">{locality}</span>}
              </div>
            )}
            {blurb && <p class="fb-blurb" data-field="footer.blurb">{blurb}</p>}
            {/* Both hooks sit on the ANCHOR: the editor's link popover reads
                data-field from whatever carries data-href-field, so a label on
                an inner span would leave it editing an empty path. The arrow is
                generated content (decision 3). */}
            {brandShowsCta && (
              <a
                class="linkc fb-cta"
                href={ctaHref}
                target={ctaExternal ? '_blank' : undefined}
                rel={ctaExternal ? 'noopener noreferrer' : undefined}
                data-field="footer.cta.text"
                data-href-field="footer.cta.href"
              >{ctaText}</a>
            )}
          </div>
        )}

        {pageLinks.length > 0 && (
          <div class="fb-col">
            {pageTitle && <h4 class="fb-h" data-field="footer.explore.title">{pageTitle}</h4>}
            {pageLinks.map((l) => (
              <a
                class="fb-row"
                href={l.href}
                data-field={`navbar_links.${l.i}.label`}
                data-href-field={`navbar_links.${l.i}.href`}
              >{l.label}</a>
            ))}
          </div>
        )}

        {hasContactCol && (
          <div class="fb-col">
            {contactTitle && <h4 class="fb-h" data-field="footer.visit.title">{contactTitle}</h4>}
            {showContactCta && (
              <a
                class="fb-row"
                href={ctaHref}
                target={ctaExternal ? '_blank' : undefined}
                rel={ctaExternal ? 'noopener noreferrer' : undefined}
                data-field="footer.cta.text"
                data-href-field="footer.cta.href"
              >{ctaText}</a>
            )}
            {(phone || email || address) && (
              <address class="fb-addr">
                {phone && (
                  <a class="fb-row fb-chan" href={`tel:${phoneDigits}`} data-field="location.phone">{phone}</a>
                )}
                {/* mailto: is BUILT, never stored — the visible text stays the
                    plain address so an inline edit can never write "mailto:…"
                    back into the email box. */}
                {email && (
                  <a class="fb-row fb-chan" href={`mailto:${email}`} data-field="contact.email">{email}</a>
                )}
                {/* The stored string verbatim — `white-space: pre-line` draws
                    the owner's own line breaks, so the DOM text and the field
                    value stay identical and a live edit cannot lie. */}
                {address && <span class="fb-addr-l" data-field="location.address">{address}</span>}
              </address>
            )}
          </div>
        )}
      </div>
    )}

    {hasColophon && (
      <div class="mono fb-colo">
        <span class="fb-id">
          {brand && <span data-field="footer.brand">{brand}</span>}
          {brand && addressFlat && <span class="fb-sep" aria-hidden="true">·</span>}
          {addressFlat && <span data-field="location.address" data-derived>{addressFlat}</span>}
        </span>
      </div>
    )}
  </div>
</footer>
)}
<style>
  /* The design's closing band: --paper-2 under one --line hairline, 58px of air
     above the grid and 38px below the colophon, exactly as drawn. The top
     figure is a clamp so a 360px screen does not open with 58px of nothing, and
     it tops out at the design's own value. The wrapper's `section` padding rule
     targets <section> only, so all of this padding is this file's. */
  .fb {
    background-color: var(--paper-2);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    padding: clamp(40px, 5.6vw, 58px) 0 38px;
  }

  /* 1.6fr / 1fr / 1fr at a 40px gap, closed by the hairline the design draws
     under it. minmax(0,…) on every track — the design's own value — so a long
     unbroken address or link label cannot widen a column past its share and
     push the page sideways. */
  .fb-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.6fr) minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 40px;
    padding-bottom: 38px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }
  .fb-brand, .fb-col { min-width: 0; }

  /* Wordmark and town tag share a baseline — the same lock as the rail, two
     sizes down (24px against the header's 26px), in --ink rather than the
     header's identical --ink. */
  .fb-lock {
    display: flex; align-items: baseline; gap: 10px; flex-wrap: wrap;
    margin-bottom: 14px; min-width: 0;
  }
  .fb-mark {
    font-family: var(--disp); font-weight: 400; font-size: 24px;
    line-height: 1.1; color: var(--ink);
    min-width: 0; overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  .fb-town {
    font-family: var(--sans); font-weight: 600; font-size: 9px;
    letter-spacing: .32em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--accent);
    min-width: 0; overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* Qualified by the parent class on purpose: the wrapper's paragraph reset is
     `html[data-page="BL"] :where(p)` at (0,1,0), and leaving this rule to win on
     the scoping attribute alone is a margin this file would rather not gamble
     on. 36ch and the 18px bottom margin are the design's own (decision 12). */
  .fb-brand .fb-blurb {
    margin: 0 0 18px;
